## Breaking Changes

<!-- If breaking changes do *not* exist, add the following: -->
<!--
There are no breaking changes in AG Charts version {% migrationVersion() %}.
-->

<!-- Otherwise -->

The full list of breaking changes across all features for version {% migrationVersion() %}.

{% expandingSection headerText="Breaking Changes" %}

This release includes the following breaking changes:

### Number Axis Interval

- A number axis with `interval.step` no longer widens its domain to make room for its labels. The domain now follows the data and the step. For example, a bar chart with x values from `0` to `11000` and `interval: { step: 100 }` now has an x domain of `-500` to `11500`, where it was `-10000` to `20000`.
- `interval.step` rounds the domain out to a multiple of the step, even when the axis is short or `interval.minSpacing` is large, so the domain can differ from version 14. For example, `interval: { step: 25 }` on a y axis 150 pixels high, with data up to `88.07`, now spans `0` to `100`, where it spanned `0` to `90`.
- A value in `interval.values` that falls outside the axis domain is no longer shown, and the domain is no longer extended to include it. For example, `interval: { values: [50, 88, 100] }` on data with a maximum of `88.07` now shows `50` and `88` on an axis from `0` to `90`, where it showed all three on an axis from `0` to `100`. A `values` array that only contains `100` shows no labels. To keep a value, extend the domain with `min`, `max`, `preferredMin` or `preferredMax`, for example `max: 100`.
- With `interval.values`, the domain no longer leaves extra room beyond the data, so a data point can sit on the edge of the series area. For example, the x axis of the Bubble with Images gallery example, with `interval: { values: [-250, 250] }`, now spans `-5000` to `2000` and the "drums" bubble at `-5000` sits on its left edge, where the axis spanned `-6000` to `2000`. To keep the space, set `min` or `preferredMin`, for example `min: -6000`.

### Radius Axis Interval

- A radius axis with `interval.step` can have a different domain when the step is very small or `interval.minSpacing` is large. For example, `interval: { step: 30, minSpacing: 400 }` on data up to `92` now spans `0` to `120`, where it spanned `0` to `100`, and `interval: { step: 1 }` now spans `0` to `92`, where it spanned `0` to `100`.

### Gradient Legend Interval

- A gradient legend with `scale.interval.step` and a large `scale.interval.minSpacing` now places its ticks at multiples of the step, so its labels can differ. For example, a legend over values from `10` to `50` with `interval: { step: 30, minSpacing: 400 }` now shows `30`, where it showed `10` and `40`.

{% /expandingSection %}

